From: Coram Engineering <eng@coram.ai>
Subject: [PATCH] fmp4mux: bound the first-stream NEED_DATA wait

In chunk mode, when nothing could be dequeued for the first stream and that
stream is neither EOS nor entirely after the current chunk, drain() returns
AGGREGATOR_FLOW_NEED_DATA to wait for more data ("advance the timeout by 1s
and hope that things are better then").

That wait is unbounded. If the oldest queued GOP cannot be dequeued for a
reason more data will not resolve, the muxer takes this exit on every
aggregate cycle: no chunk is started, no fragment is started, nothing is
pushed downstream, and the input queue grows without bound.

Observed on a live H.264+AAC pipeline (fragment-duration=2s,
chunk-duration=100ms): 373 drains entered in 60s, 385 NEED_DATA returns,
zero "Starting new chunk"/"Starting new fragment", and over three minutes of
GOPs queued and still growing while the muxer emitted nothing. The stream
never recovers.

Bound the wait: once far more than a fragment is already queued, more data
cannot help, so fall through to the generic "nothing dequeued" handling and
let the chunk and fragment advance instead of stalling forever.

Also log the otherwise silent early return in drain_buffers_one_stream(),
which made this failure mode very hard to attribute -- it is the only exit
producing no buffers that emitted no trace at all.
--- a/mux/fmp4/src/fmp4mux/imp.rs
+++ b/mux/fmp4/src/fmp4mux/imp.rs
@@ -1982,6 +1982,13 @@
 
         let mut gops = Vec::with_capacity(stream.queued_gops.len());
         if stream.queued_gops.is_empty() || stream.late_gop {
+            gst::debug!(
+                CAT,
+                obj = stream.sinkpad,
+                "Draining nothing for this stream (no queued GOPs: {}, late GOP: {})",
+                stream.queued_gops.is_empty(),
+                stream.late_gop,
+            );
             stream.late_gop = false;
             return Ok(gops);
         }
@@ -2751,8 +2758,35 @@
                                 chunk_start_pts + settings.chunk_duration.unwrap()
                             }
                     });
+                    // Waiting for more data only helps if more data can make this stream
+                    // drainable. Once far more than a fragment is queued it cannot:
+                    // something is preventing the oldest GOP from being dequeued, and
+                    // returning NEED_DATA here waits forever - the muxer stops emitting
+                    // while its queue grows without bound. Bound the wait and fall through
+                    // to the generic "nothing dequeued" handling so the chunk and fragment
+                    // can still advance.
+                    let queued_duration =
+                        Option::zip(stream.queued_gops.front(), stream.queued_gops.back())
+                            .map(|(newest, oldest)| {
+                                newest.end_pts.saturating_sub(oldest.start_pts)
+                            })
+                            .unwrap_or(gst::ClockTime::ZERO);
+                    // More than twice the fragment duration, expressed with saturating_sub
+                    // to avoid overflow on the addition.
+                    let queued_far_past_fragment = queued_duration
+                        .saturating_sub(settings.fragment_duration)
+                        > settings.fragment_duration;
+
                     if stream.sinkpad.is_eos() || stream_after_chunk {
                         // This is handled below generally if nothing was dequeued
+                    } else if queued_far_past_fragment {
+                        gst::warning!(
+                            CAT,
+                            obj = stream.sinkpad,
+                            "Nothing to drain for the first stream but {} queued (fragment duration {}); not waiting for more data",
+                            queued_duration,
+                            settings.fragment_duration,
+                        );
                     } else {
                         if settings.chunk_duration.is_some() {
                             gst::debug!(
